            <ArticleTitle>[Focus on Achalasia].</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0755-4982(17)30369-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.lpm.2017.08.010</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The pathophysiology of achalasia is largely unknown, and involves the destruction of ganglion cell in the esophageal myenteric plexus. High-resolution esophageal manometry is the key investigation. Endoscopic pneumodilatation and laparoscopic Heller myotomy have comparable short-term success rates, around 90%. The main complication after pneumodilatation is esophageal perforation, occurring in about 1% of cases. Peroral endoscopic myotomy is a promising treatment modality, however with frequent post-procedural gastroesophageal reflux.</AbstractText>
